ime open the package JSON  create an object called engines then set  node to a string value of eight then  another thing you want to do if you're  using typescript is add the Dom Lib to  your TS config this is purely for  tooling and it will just give us  autocomplete on anything related to the  Dom that we're doing inside of puppeteer  and the last piece of setup is to  install puppeteer with NPM  you'll notice that that downloads  chromium which is over a hundred  megabytes in size and chrome in general  requires a higher memory allocation than  the typical 250 megabytes that you would  find in a function so we're going to  beef that up in the next step let's go  ahead and open up the indexed es file  and then we'll import puppeteer in  addition to firebase functions the first  thing I want to show you is how to visit  a page with puppeteer serialized the  HTML to a string then send that as the  response from the function but the first  thing we'll do is set up our function to  run with at least one gigabyte of memory  then after that we can set up our HTTP  function which will be on request giving  us a request and response object which  we can also make an async function the  very first thing you'll do with  puppeteer is create a browser instance  which you can do with puppeteer launch  and then there's a number of different  configuration options that you can pass  in to launch in this case we'll run in  headless mode and I also found that you  need the know sandbox flag for this to  work properly so that's the equivalent  of opening up a browser the next thing  we'll do is create a new page which can  be thought of as the equivalent of a new  tab in the browser and everything in  puppeteer is a sink so you'll notice  that I'm awaiting all of these methods  the next step is to navigate to an  actual URL in this case I'm navigating  to the ionic app that I built which is  just a very simple set of pages when you  navigate to a page it's going to execute  all that JavaScript and you can control  how long puppeteer waits in this case  I'm setting it to network idle zero  which just means it'll wait for network  requests to be idle for 500 milliseconds  and then render the page there at this  point there's all kinds of different  things we can do we might want to scrape  content from the page we might want to  inject jQuery and make manipulations to  it we can take a screen shot and do  pretty much anything else you can do in  a regular web browser but if you just  need the rendered content which is all  we need here we can say a wait page  content and then we'll send that as the  response from this HTTP function now  we'll go ahead and serve this function  and see if it's rendering the meta tags  that we have set up on our front end  application so I'm using an ionic 4 app  here which has an interesting caveat  with web components anything that uses  the shadow Dom is really hard to  serialized back to regular HTML so in  this demo we're only going to use  puppeteer to render meta tags so we can  display our content on social media  sites like Facebook and Twitter but  that's really only a concern if your app  uses the shadow Dom because basically  what we've built here is a proxy browser  that allows us to visit a web page  without technically visiting that web  page on our own browser right now I'm  looking at the rendered content for  angular firebase.com but I never  actually went to this website instead we  went to the cloud functions middleman  which rendered the page with puppeteer  on the server and then sent me that  serialized content as the response so  you basically now have server-side  rendering for any web site on the  Internet  but things aren't quite that simple in  the real world in the full source code  for this project  I've poured it over the render code from  render Tron which handles a lot of the  little things that you'll need to do  when rendering pages things like setting  the base href for your static resources  handling errors loading polyfills for  web components and stuff like that now  the last thing I want to do is rewrite  our functions so we can set up  server-side rendering with firebase  hosting so we'll import the serialize  function from render tron then we'll set  up an app URL which is the location of  where your app is deployed on firebase  hosting or it might be localhost 5004  local testing then we'll write a cloud  function specifically for rendering  pages with puppeteer this first render  function is going to look almost  identical to the one that we just wrote  but instead of using page content we'll  use our serialize method so we have a  little more sophisticated rendering  process and for this endpoint will allow  the render URL to come through as a  query parameter meaning it can be passed  directly through the URL and the  serialize method will respond with the  page content as well as a status code  which we can just go ahead and send back  as the response from this function the  next function we'll write is called SSR  for server-side rendering and we're  going to configure our hosting account  to reroute all requests to this function  endpoint then what we want to do is  determine whether or not the request is  coming from a bot or a human if it's a  bot we're going to want to render all  the content on the server so we have the  meta tags and any other  dynamic content loaded into the page I  have a list of common BOTS here and then  we can see the bots on the request by  looking at the user agent header then  I'll run a filter on our list of bots to  see if there's any from this list  included in the user agent header so if  the length is zero then we know we're  not dealing with a bot but if it's  greater than zero we know that we should  render this page with puppeteer if the  user agent is a bot which would happen  if someone posted this link and social  media then we want to go ahead and  render the page fully with render tron  we can do that by calling the render  function that we created previously that  handles all the puppeteer stuff so we'll  just go ahead and await the response  from that endpoint which should be the  serialized content from the page then we  can take that HTML and send it as the  response body from this function  otherwise we want to just fetch the  route URL from the hosting account and  send that as the response back a single  page application will get this response  and handle the URL client side so it'll  behave just as if the user visited this  page normally on a regular hosting  account you can either deploy this  function or run it locally then at this  point I'm assuming that you already have  a firebase hosting account setup and if  so you'll have it set up as a single  page application that looks something  like this where you're routing the  source to an index.html file we're gonna  go ahead and change that to a function  and have it route to the SSR function  that we just wrote then the final step  is to run firebase deploy which will  deploy both the functions and the  hosting for your client side app and as  a normal visitor to your app you  shouldn't really notice any major  changes but only if you post to a link  bot such as slack or Twitter or whatever  it should intercept that request and  display the meta tags necessary for rich  social media cards so that's how you set  up puppeteer in a cloud function and  we're really just barely scratching the  surface there's all kinds of cool things  you can do one of which is the headless  chrome crawler which is an open source  project built on top a puppeteer that  allows you to iterate over the links in  a web page run jobs in parallel export  to CSV and a bunch of other advanced  stuff I'm gonna go ahead and wrap it up  there if you want to see more advanced  puppeteer content leave me a comment  down below and if you want to take your  development to the next level consider  becoming a pro member at